P6 — CI wiring for the agentic security layer (Path B).

`tools/security_audit.py` now asserts the five L9 controls in addition to
the original eight-layer battery, and `tests/test_sec_agentic_audit.py`
proves it turns red under twenty sabotage scenarios. The audit is already
wired into CI by `docs/handoff/sec-audit.patch` (the `Security audit`
step), so an owner who applied that patch gets the new coverage for free
on the next run — no workflow change is required for the audit itself.

This patch adds the two things that patch does not cover:

1. the L9 sabotage suite as its own named step, so a broken alarm is
   distinguishable in the CI log from a broken control;
2. the offline probe as a smoke check, so `tools/sec_agentic_probe.py`
   cannot rot silently.

Apply with:

    git apply docs/handoff/sec-agentic-audit.patch

If `docs/handoff/sec-audit.patch` has not been applied yet, apply it
first — this patch anchors on the `Security audit` step it introduces.

--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -46,6 +46,12 @@
 
       - name: Security audit
         run: python tools/security_audit.py
+
+      - name: Agentic security alarm (L9 sabotage suite)
+        run: python -m pytest tests/test_sec_agentic_audit.py -q
+
+      - name: Agentic security probe (offline smoke)
+        run: python tools/sec_agentic_probe.py
       # Keep pytest isolated from frontend/Rust builds in this job. Telegram
       # timing assertions are sensitive to CPU contention and can flake when a
       # developer runs a concurrent build; each CI job has its own runner.
